### What is the visibility of each modifier?

| Modifier | Same class | Same package | Subclass (other package) | Other package |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| `public` | Yes | Yes | Yes | Yes |
| `protected` | Yes | Yes | Yes | No |
| default (none) | Yes | Yes | No | No |
| `private` | Yes | No | No | No |

### How does each modifier work in code?

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class Account {
    public String accountId;          // visible everywhere
    protected String holderName;      // visible in package + subclasses
    String branchCode;                // package-private (no keyword)
    private BigDecimal balance;       // visible only inside Account

    public BigDecimal getBalance() {   // public getter
        return balance;
    }

    private void validateBalance() {   // only Account can call this
        if (balance.signum() < 0) {
            throw new IllegalStateException("Negative balance");
        }
    }
}
```

```java
package com.bank.core;  // SAME package

public class TransferService {
    public void transfer(Account from, Account to, BigDecimal amount) {
        // public field: accessible
        System.out.println(from.accountId);

        // protected field: accessible (same package)
        System.out.println(from.holderName);

        // package-private field: accessible (same package)
        System.out.println(from.branchCode);

        // private field: NOT accessible
        // System.out.println(from.balance);  // COMPILE ERROR
        System.out.println(from.getBalance());  // use public getter

        // private method: NOT accessible
        // from.validateBalance();  // COMPILE ERROR
    }
}
```

```java
package com.bank.mobile;  // DIFFERENT package

import com.bank.core.Account;

public class MobileApp {
    public void display(Account account) {
        // public: accessible
        System.out.println(account.accountId);

        // protected: NOT accessible (different package, not a subclass)
        // System.out.println(account.holderName);  // COMPILE ERROR

        // package-private: NOT accessible
        // System.out.println(account.branchCode);  // COMPILE ERROR

        // private: NOT accessible
        // System.out.println(account.balance);  // COMPILE ERROR
    }
}
```

### How does protected work with subclasses?

Protected fields are accessible to subclasses even in different packages:

```java
package com.bank.savings;  // DIFFERENT package

import com.bank.core.Account;

public class SavingsAccount extends Account {
    public void showHolder() {
        // protected: accessible because SavingsAccount is a subclass
        System.out.println(this.holderName);  // works!

        // package-private: NOT accessible (different package)
        // System.out.println(this.branchCode);  // COMPILE ERROR
    }
}
```

Important: the subclass can access `protected` members on `this` or on instances of itself. It cannot access them on an arbitrary `Account` reference from a different package.

### What access modifiers are allowed on top-level classes?

Top-level classes (not nested inside another class) can only be:

- **public** – visible to all packages. The file must be named after the class.
- **package-private (default)** – visible only within its own package. No keyword needed.

```java
// OK: public top-level class
public class Account { ... }

// OK: package-private top-level class
class InternalHelper { ... }

// NOT OK: private top-level class
// private class Secret { ... }  // COMPILE ERROR

// NOT OK: protected top-level class
// protected class SemiPublic { ... }  // COMPILE ERROR
```

Private and protected only make sense for members (fields, methods, inner classes) that are inside another class. A top-level class has no enclosing class to be private or protected relative to.

### What access modifiers are allowed on nested/inner classes?

Nested classes can use all four modifiers:

```java
public class Account {
    public static class Builder { ... }          // public nested
    protected static class Validator { ... }     // protected nested
    static class Formatter { ... }               // package-private nested
    private static class Cache { ... }           // private nested
}
```

### What is the correct encapsulation pattern for banking entities?

```java
public class Account {
    // ALL fields private – never expose state directly
    private final String accountId;
    private BigDecimal balance;
    private boolean active;

    // Constructor validates inputs
    public Account(String accountId, BigDecimal initialBalance) {
        this.accountId = Objects.requireNonNull(accountId);
        this.balance = initialBalance;
        this.active = true;
    }

    // Public methods control access with validation
    public void debit(BigDecimal amount) {
        if (amount.signum() <= 0) throw new IllegalArgumentException("Positive amount required");
        if (balance.compareTo(amount) < 0) throw new IllegalStateException("Insufficient funds");
        balance = balance.subtract(amount);
    }

    // Read-only access
    public BigDecimal getBalance() { return balance; }
    public String getAccountId() { return accountId; }
    public boolean isActive() { return active; }

    // Internal helper – private, not part of the API
    private void audit(String action, BigDecimal amount) {
        // log for internal audit trail
    }
}
```

Rule: fields are always `private`. Methods that external code calls are `public`. Methods used only within the class are `private`. Methods used within the package are default (package-private).

### What are common mistakes?

- **Making fields public.** `public BigDecimal balance` lets anyone set it to a negative number. Use `private` + controlled methods.
- **Using protected when default is enough.** Protected is for inheritance across packages. If you do not need that, use default.
- **Confusing default with public.** If no modifier is written, the member is package-private (NOT public). `void transfer()` is only visible in the same package.
- **Accessing protected via a different class reference.** A subclass in another package can access `this.protectedField` but not `someOtherAccount.protectedField`.

### What should a senior engineer know?

- **Modules (Java 9+) add another layer.** Even public classes must be `exports`-ed from their module to be visible to other modules. A public class in a non-exported package is invisible outside the module.
- **Interfaces: methods are implicitly public.** You cannot make an interface method private (before Java 9) or package-private. Java 9+ allows private methods in interfaces for code reuse within default methods.
- **Reflection can bypass access modifiers.** `field.setAccessible(true)` makes a private field accessible. But Java modules restrict this via `--add-opens`.
- **Records: components generate public getters.** A record component `String accountId` generates a public `accountId()` method. The component itself is private final.
- **Encapsulation is not just about getters.** A `setBalance(BigDecimal)` method is almost as bad as a public field. Provide behavior methods (`debit`, `credit`) instead of setters.
